I have enabled Row Level Security (RLS) on all five tables (users, channels, posts, comments, and channel_moderators) and created the corresponding policies according to your RLS requirements:

1. Users Table:
   - SELECT: All users can read public profiles.
   - UPDATE: Users can update only their own profile.
   - DELETE: Users can delete only their own account.

2. Channels Table:
   - SELECT: Everyone can read public channel info.
   - INSERT: Authenticated users can create channels.
   - UPDATE: Only channel owners can update.
   - DELETE: Only channel owners can delete.

3. Posts Table:
   - SELECT: Users can read posts in channels they have access to (public channels or they are owner/moderator).
   - INSERT: Authenticated users can create posts.
   - UPDATE: Post authors or channel moderators/owners can edit.
   - DELETE: Post authors or channel moderators/owners can delete.

4. Comments Table:
   - SELECT: Users can read comments on accessible posts.
   - INSERT: Authenticated users can comment on posts they can see.
   - UPDATE: Comment authors, post authors, or channel moderators/owners can edit.
   - DELETE: Comment authors, post authors, or channel moderators/owners can delete.

5. Channel Moderators Table:
   - SELECT: Everyone can view moderator lists.
   - INSERT: Only channel owners can add moderators.
   - DELETE: Channel owners can remove moderators; moderators can remove themselves.

All policies leverage the provided helper functions (app_current_user_id(), is_channel_owner(), is_channel_moderator()) and follow the schema and performance constraints you outlined.

